Make One Guarded Change with ProChat Workbench

**Status:** draft requiring manual review before recording or publication  
**Product lane:** ProChat Workbench  
**Bounded outcome:** change one approved file, validate it, and leave unrelated work untouched

Today I’m going to make one small repository change with ProChat Workbench.

And I’m going to show the controls before I show the edit.

The result is simple.

One approved file changes.

The targeted validation passes.

Unrelated work remains untouched and unstaged.

That is the entire outcome.

My name is Steve.

I spent more than 12 years testing software.

The habit that matters here is not trusting a result because it looks reasonable.

We need visible evidence for scope and behavior.

Let’s begin with the before-state.

I have a sanitized repository with one pre-existing unrelated change.

That unrelated file is not part of today’s task.

I show Git status before asking Workbench to do anything.

This gives us evidence we can compare later.

Now I define the task.

Update one sentence in the project README.

Modify exactly that path.

Do not change application files.

Do not stage or commit.

Run the smallest relevant documentation check afterward.

That task has one outcome and one approved path.

Before editing, Workbench reads the exact current file.

This protects us from changing an outdated or imagined version of the text.

Now ChatGPT proposes the wording.

The proposed wording is an interpretation of the task.

The current file remains the source of truth for where the change belongs.

Workbench applies the bounded edit.

Now I stop and inspect the changed-path list.

I expect two modified files in the worktree.

The unrelated file that already existed.

And the one README file changed for this task.

If another path appears, the task is not clean.

I do not continue by hoping the extra change is harmless.

I inspect or revert it before moving forward.

In this demonstration, the changed-path list matches the expected state.

Next, I review the diff for the approved file.

The diff shows one sentence changed.

No surrounding section was rewritten.

No unrelated wording moved.

No hidden formatting change appeared.

Now I run the targeted validation.

The check confirms that the document still satisfies its required structure.

A passing check is evidence for that specific rule.

It is not proof that every part of the project is correct.

That distinction matters.

Now I check Git status again.

The pre-existing unrelated file is still modified.

The approved README is also modified.

Nothing is staged.

Nothing is committed.

The unrelated work was not overwritten, included, or silently cleaned up.

That is the control point for this video.

A clean task does not require a clean worktree.

It requires a verified boundary between task work and unrelated work.

Now I show the final evidence side by side.

The approved path.

The exact diff.

The passing targeted check.

And the final staged state.

This task is complete because those pieces agree.

Not because the sentence looks better.

Not because ChatGPT said it was done.

The limitation is clear.

This demonstration proves one guarded documentation change.

It does not prove that every kind of repository task has the same risk.

Larger changes need broader evidence and different validation.

The next action is simple.

Try one guarded task on a repository you are allowed to use.

Name the exact paths that may change.

State what must remain untouched.

And decide before the task whether anything may be staged or committed.

Before recording or publishing this script, the repository example, exact commands, validation result, and visible worktree state must be reviewed manually.
